  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 124 PM MDT Sat Jul 25 2026\n\nHigh pressure aloft will remain entrenched over much of the southern \nRockies and southern deserts through the next several days with only \nminor fluctuations in strength and orientation. Currently, PWATs \nremain between 1.0 and 1.3 inch over much of the forecast area, and \nthrough early to middle of next week the orientation of the high \nwill keep monsoon moisture favoring Sonora into southern AZ with \nmodified and more subdued PWATs into NM and far west TX. Sluggish \nstorm motions will be common, increasing the locally heavy rainfall \nthreat through at least early next week.\n\nToday's storms will be mostly concentrated over the Sacramento \nmountains and Gila wilderness through mid afternoon before\npropagating into nearby valleys with storms struggling to \ninitiate farther south near the borderland. The Flood Watch for \nthe Sacramento's will end at 6 PM MDT when the heavy rainfall \nthreat will abate there. \n\nInto Sunday, high resolution models indicate the Gila and \nSacramento's observing convective initiation again, while storms \nalso develop farther southwest over the boot heel and along the \nSonora- Chihuahua border. Consequently, isolated to scattered \nstorms have been built into the Sunday afternoon forecast for much\nof the western half of the forecast area with most of far west TX\nstaying void of storms. Temperatures will follow close to \npersistence with lots of upper 90's and low 100's in the lower \nelevation deserts. \n\nChanges into Monday are subtle with a slight increase in storm \ncoverage over western areas where cells will continue to slowly \ndrift westward. Temperatures will gain a degree or two of warming, \nespecially along the Rio Grande. \n\nMinimal changes in the strength or orientation of the high Tuesday \nonward will make it difficult to pinpoint any appreciable day-to-day \nchanges with sensible weather elements. POPs mimic climatology \nfor late July, typically the most active thunderstorm period, with\nthe Gila, NM boot heel, and Sacramento's staying the favored \nareas for rainfall. Temperatures in the Rio Grande will escalate \nto 100 to 105 in many locales, increasing heat impacts next week.",
